00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,240
But once you have a somewhat usable app.

215
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:13,840
app, were you using it?

216
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:17,040
Sounds like you already had a kind of a group of people that were then using it.

217
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:18,880
Yeah, it was a fairly small group.

218
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:20,240
I wanted to keep it.

219
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:31,280
Obviously, I didn't have really anyone to, it's not like I had this huge audience I was keeping it from, but I did have just some family testing it really while it was maturing.

220
00:16:31,760 --> 00:16:37,040
I didn't want to have anyone using it who might be broken by some future data model change.

221
00:16:37,280 --> 00:16:37,760
So yeah.

222
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:46,800
I think that was the I kept it very small and warned heavily that this was something you shouldn't rely on until it was ready.

223
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:48,960
But it was quite useful because it was a very good thing.

224
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:53,360
It was quite useful because my husband's very techie, so he obviously looks at things in a similar way.

225
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:56,640
But my dad is a different user, I guess.

226
00:16:57,200 --> 00:16:59,280
And I did what I wanted.

227
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:08,240
I wanted ideally Chronicling to appeal also to people who are not going to install shortcuts and, you know, modify the settings and whatever.

228
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:09,280
So it could be used.

229
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:13,920
But both ends of the user spectrum, I suppose, is what I was hoping to achieve.

230
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:14,320
Yeah.

231
00:17:15,120 --> 00:17:31,440
I mean, we haven't fully described the app yet, but like, we've kind of given the impression the way we're talking that it's in the same category as these sort of shortcuts extension apps because it kind of has that feeling to it and definitely appeals to a similar crowd, but it is very much its own standalone app.

232
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:34,160
It's not like it's dependent on shortcuts at all, right?

233
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:46,640
Oh yeah, I think you could use it just through particularly I'm not sure when this is going to air, but when interactive widgets and things come in a few weeks time, you could easily use the app without ever really opening it.

234
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:56,720
But equally, the intention is that you can open the app and have a couple of views that represent a couple of different ways of looking at the data together.

235
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,560
So, so yeah, it's it's meant to it's meant to achieveieve both things, ideally.

236
00:18:03,040 --> 00:18:03,120
Okay.

237
00:18:03,360 --> 00:18:12,880
So you have a group, you're getting feedback, it sounds like, uh, from kind of different, uh, segments of the market, uh, even with the, the small group that you have.

238
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:16,640
And I guess that is feeding into your development then.

239
00:18:16,800 --> 00:18:28,640
And like, uh, did you get that kind of cycle where someone would give you feedback, which gives you an idea, and you'd implement that and send it back out and kind of get in that nice cycle of, uh, improvement at that stage?

240
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:29,600
Uh, yeah.

241
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:36,720
I think it was, it was really helpful to get, um, both feedback in terms of feature ideas as such, but also bugs.

242
00:18:37,360 --> 00:18:42,320
and not just obvious bugs, but just things like if you've used it for a few months, the scale grows.

243
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:50,800
And it's quite, it's an app that, yeah, it's, it's some things you may only find when you've used it hundreds or thousands of times.

244
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:56,960
So it was quite helpful to just have, have that scale with me personally.

245
00:18:57,120 --> 00:19:01,360
I remember setting up, kind of, whenever an app is launched, type automations.

246
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:06,080
So with quite a few data points, depending on which app I chose.

247
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:08,720
Those are the ones that I have set up as well.

248
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:10,800
So okay.

249
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:11,360
So then.

250
00:19:13,280 --> 00:19:16,960
What was the lead up to the actual launch then?

251
00:19:18,240 --> 00:19:21,200
I guess there's a couple of different questions sort of buried in here.

252
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:28,480
So one is like, did you expand the test flight, you know, the beta group at all?

253
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:34,240
And then how did you like decide when, you know, when go live was going to be?

254
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:35,680
Yeah.

255
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:41,920
So I did expand the test flight onto a very I announced a test flight on on Mastodon.

256
00:19:42,320 --> 00:19:50,000
I think it was an interesting time because the service formerly known as Twitter had obviously kind of imploded relatively recently.

257
00:19:50,640 --> 00:19:54,960
And then Reddit as well was, shall we say, undergoing changes.

258
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:00,560
So, so there were this kind of audience was very different.

259
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:08,240
But in some ways I found that beneficial because I actually really appreciated the smaller but more focused community on Mastodon.

260
00:20:08,560 --> 00:20:23,200
So, I, you know, I used I used a couple of the SwiftUI hashtags and I found I managed to find kind of, I think, a couple of dozen beta testers who were extremely helpful in providing some more feedback.

261
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:24,960
And so that's it.

262
00:20:24,960 --> 00:20:27,520
So that happened over the kind of spring and early summer.

263
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:38,400
As for actual launch, I didn't really have a defined set of features, but I was trying to target, um, July kind of after WTC basically.

264
00:20:38,720 --> 00:20:49,120
Um, so I was doing my just trying to get through as much as I could before that stage, um, and aiming for that, that window of time, I guess, in the, in the Apple calendar.

265
00:20:49,920 --> 00:20:56,880
I think that's a really helpful way to approach, you know, first time apps is like targeting a date.

266
00:20:57,040 --> 00:21:02,160
It's hard when it's an a couple of different things.

267
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:07,920
There's one, it's like, it's hard to define, like, where is the V one line?

268
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:11,680
And there's this feeling that this is like your one shot.

269
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:13,760
And so you don't want to blow it, right?

270
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:25,200
And so I think, I think by setting a date, it forces you to actually figure out, like, okay, if I'm going to cut off at this time, what has to be in here?

271
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:26,960
Or I'm going to be so embarrassed.

272
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:28,560
I can't possibly release this.

273
00:21:28,960 --> 00:21:32,720
And it kind of forces you to, like, work backwards from that date to where you are.

274
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:38,720
And the reality is you get, you know, 90% of that work done in the couple of weeks up to that date.

275
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:40,000
But I don't know.

276
00:21:40,240 --> 00:21:41,920
It feels like that's a really good way to do it.

277
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:47,200
I I did actually it's very, it's very weird how many parallels there are to dark noise here, but I did the same thing.

278
00:21:47,360 --> 00:21:59,520
I was trying to like thread the needle between, uh, between WWDC and the actual iPhone event, um, because that felt like it was a little bit of a lull time for a lot of things.

279
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:07,920
Um, and it gives you, I wasn't thinking about it this strategically, but it gives you a nice secondary bump with the iOS release.

280
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:12,800
So you kind of get two new cycles to play in and some natural features you can add to the app afterwards.

281
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:14,640
So that's pretty cool.

282
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:17,600
Okay, so you picked the date in July.

283
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:25,840
You had, you know, some personal people in a beta, and then that grew, that grew as you sort of marketed it online.

284
00:22:26,320 --> 00:22:26,800
Oh, yeah.

285
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:46,880
And to that point about Mastodon, I think it's, there's a really, it's a really scary thing for people who are established whenever a social network, a club, a coffee house, I don't know, whatever it is in the real life parallel, when something like that that's outside of your control starts kind of collapsing, it's scary if you have something established.

286
00:22:47,040 --> 00:22:51,120
Like how do you, how do you move this growth that you made to another area?

287
00:22:51,680 --> 00:23:04,240
But if you aren't already established, it's like a huge opportunity because I know for me, there's a lot of people I started following on Mastodon that have been in the Twitter sphere, successful.

288
00:23:04,480 --> 00:23:06,640
Somehow, they just weren't on my radar for a long time.

289
00:23:06,960 --> 00:23:11,600
But whenever you jump over to a new platform, you're just like looking around for people that are interesting.

290
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:13,120
And so it's kind of a good opportunity.

291
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:19,440
I think right now, if threads is the thing that's going to take off, people are doing the same thing there.

292
00:23:19,600 --> 00:23:22,720
They're trying to fill up their feed with interesting people.

293
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:25,040
And so I do think there's something.

294
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:32,160
I think there's going to be a lot of people whose origin stories start with, you know, these kind of transitions that happen.

295
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:34,960
So, yeah, I think that's I think that's cool.

296
00:23:35,680 --> 00:23:35,760
Okay.

297
00:23:35,920 --> 00:23:37,600
So we're leading up to the launch.

298
00:23:38,240 --> 00:23:41,840
What were you doing to try and make this launch a success?

299
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:49,840
I mean, from my perspective, the launch seemed like it was a huge success, but I'm curious, were you doing like marketing pushes leading up to it?

300
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:52,800
Uh, were you mostly leveraging sort of your beta group?

301
00:23:53,120 --> 00:23:56,240
Like, how were you kind of making this, this day, uh, a big thing?

302
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:12,720
Uh, I think the line in the sand date as such, which was really helpful because, particularly as a first time, an indie developer without professional experience, realizing quite a lot of work comes beyond the technical, was slowly dawning on me.

303
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:20,640
Things like the extent of the App Store submission, preparing marketing assets and a basket, and that was all very new to me.

304
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:29,040
So that was a big part of the process to learn and learn a huge amount from previous people and launched on this.

305
00:24:29,200 --> 00:24:36,640
Actually, hearing people like Sean Hickman talk about and his excellent YouTube channel talk about their kind of process was really helpful.

306
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:45,440
And then I think getting towards the day itself, I did reach out to a few people in the press.

307
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:53,920
I think it's extremely daunting to do that, sending emails to people you've been following for a long time.

308
00:24:54,480 --> 00:24:54,800
Oh yeah.

309
00:24:55,440 --> 00:25:04,080
But I think it's just one of the things I had to kind of decide if I'm going to do this, then I need to push through the perceived awkwardness of that.

310
00:25:04,560 --> 00:25:04,640
Yeah.

311
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:12,960
Well, the episode that, as we're recording, aired yesterday, I think, was with Chance Miller, the nine to five MAC editor and chief.

312
00:25:13,280 --> 00:25:17,840
And like we talked about that where it's like from our perspective, we all hate email.

313
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:23,680
It feels like this horrible thing to do to someone to put an email, add an email to their inbox.

314
00:25:24,560 --> 00:25:26,000
But he talked about from his perspective.

315
00:25:26,160 --> 00:25:31,760
It's like, yeah, they get a lot and they can't keep up with all of it, but also that's just necessary for their jobs.

316
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
Like they want to be getting that from people.

317
00:25:34,240 --> 00:25:40,000
And so, yeah, I try to keep that in mind when I'm doing the same thing where I'm like, yeah, it just feels like spammy.

318
00:25:41,520 --> 00:25:48,160
So, yeah, I did, I did send those, I sent a few emails, and I continued to share some screenshots and things like that on Mastodon.

319
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:57,120
I did also open a pre-order, which also feels a bit strange for a free app, but it seems to be something that sometimes people do.

320
00:25:57,360 --> 00:26:14,240
So once the app was approved, I think I set about a week in advance for the actual release date and started to share that link again on Mastodon, and that generated quite a bit of mini buzz in the mini iOS circle, I guess.

321
00:26:15,440 --> 00:26:16,080
So yeah.

322
00:26:16,720 --> 00:26:21,520
Okay, that's interesting because to be honest with you, I kind of forgot about the pre-order thing.

323
00:26:22,160 --> 00:26:27,840
I did that with Dark Noise and I remember I think I even wrote about, like, I recommend people do this, but I was a paid up front app.

324
00:26:28,160 --> 00:26:30,400
So it's kind of a different whole ordeal.

325
00:26:31,280 --> 00:26:39,280
But that's, that's an interesting idea because, yeah, it gives, it gives people a call to action throughout that week while you're building Buzz.

326
00:26:39,760 --> 00:26:47,040
And then all of those, it's almost like you're giving everybody, like, sign up for my newsletter because they're going to get a notification on the day it actually comes out.

327
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:49,680
So you feel like that, that worked out pretty well?

328
00:26:50,880 --> 00:26:51,280
I think so.

329
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:51,520
Yeah.

330
00:26:51,840 --> 00:26:55,760
I haven't kind of deeply analyzed the numbers, um, but there certainly were some pre orders.

331
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:59,200
And, uh, I guess it gives you two shots at the launch almost.

332
00:26:59,280 --> 00:26:59,360
Yeah.

333
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:02,640
So kind of announcing the pre order and then the actual launch itself.

334
00:27:03,440 --> 00:27:07,760
It also alerted me to a slight issue with the inappe purchase pricing.

335
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:15,760
So I don't know quite why this happened, but I'd set the prices in British sterling and then set the automated currency conversion.

336
00:27:15,920 --> 00:27:16,000
Yeah.

337
00:27:16,480 --> 00:27:26,240
And for whatever reason, the annual price in certain territories had worked out more expensive than the monthly times twelve price, which obviously was not my intention.

338
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:30,080
I've heard I've heard of people saying that that happens.

339
00:27:30,240 --> 00:27:30,320
Yeah.

340
00:27:30,560 --> 00:27:33,680
And I don't I don't remember what the deal is with that.

341
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,640
I work for an inappe purchase subscription company.

342
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:38,880
I should probably know the answer to this, but I don't.

343
00:27:39,280 --> 00:27:41,840
I know I've heard this story actually, maybe it was you talking about it.

344
00:27:42,320 --> 00:27:43,920
I know I've heard this story before though.

345
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:46,560
I don't remember what the reason is for that.

346
00:27:47,280 --> 00:27:57,120
And it might just literally be, I know the conversion rates are weird for certain countries for reasons that I don't really understand, so maybe it's just something specific with that.

347
00:27:57,440 --> 00:27:58,960
Maybe Dark Sound has that same problem.

348
00:27:59,200 --> 00:27:59,600
I don't know.

349
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:01,680
But I haven't heard anything about that.

350
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:02,880
Yeah, that's interesting.

351
00:28:03,520 --> 00:28:06,880
So you heard about it because people reached out and told you basically.

352
00:28:07,440 --> 00:28:10,880
Yeah, someone sent me a message, I think, from Sweden where this had happened.

353
00:28:11,360 --> 00:28:16,640
And it gave me an opportunity to to go and download the spreadsheet of all the prices and double check each one.

354
00:28:16,960 --> 00:28:24,880
So I think it's I read somewhere that it was about the currency conversion is handled differently for a kind of a small versus a large amount or something like that.

355
00:28:26,480 --> 00:28:32,960
But yeah, it was certainly an unintended byproduct of the pre order meant that I didn't end up with some very strange subscription.

356
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:33,440
Yeah.

357
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:41,760
Well, I mean, I know they're not it's not like it's a direct conversion because, you know, they set the pricing based on optimization of some kind, right?

358
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:48,480
Like you want it to be 99 cents and then you don't necessarily want it to be 1,200.

359
00:28:50,400 --> 00:28:50,960
It's not sense.

360
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:51,920
You know what I'm saying?

361
00:28:52,160 --> 00:28:58,080
Like, they want it to be at a certain, you know, digit number just for marketing reasons.

362
00:28:58,960 --> 00:29:00,560
And they're trying to automatically do that for you.

363
00:29:00,640 --> 00:29:02,240
So maybe it's something related to that.

364
00:29:02,400 --> 00:29:02,800
I don't know.

365
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:03,920
That's interesting.

